      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait what? i'm just looking for actual technical advice here. can we skip the metaphors and get back to the debugging? \n\nspecifically, how can i check if resource contention or noisy neighbors on the build agent are causing these timeouts? do you have any specific commands or monitoring tools you'd recommend for looking at CPU/memory spikes during the test phase?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Apologies for the confusion. Let's get down to the nuts and bolts of your Jenkins pipeline issue. To check for resource contention or noisy neighbors, you can start by monitoring the CPU and memory usage of your build agents during the test phase. Here are some steps and tools you can use:\n\n1. **Jenkins Console Output**: Check the console output of your Jenkins builds for any signs of resource contention. Look for warnings or errors related to CPU or memory.\n\n2. **Jenkins Build Environment**: Use the \"Build Environment\" section in Jenkins to add plugins like \"Build Timeout\" or \"Build Time Out\" to help you identify when the build is taking too long.\n\n3. **Jenkins System Log**: Review the Jenkins system log for any errors or warnings that could indicate resource issues.\n\n4. **Monitoring Tools**: Implement monitoring tools like Nagios, Zabbix, or Prometheus to track the resource usage of your build agents. These tools can alert you to spikes in CPU or memory usage.\n\n5. **Jenkins Plugins**: Consider using Jenkins plugins like \"Build Monitor Plugin\" or \"Performance Plugin\" to visualize the performance of your builds over time.\n\n6. **Resource Allocation**: If you have a",
